To handle the `InputMismatchException` in this code, you can add input validation and error handling to ensure that the user enters an integer value before trying to read it using `Scanner`. Here's an updated implementation:

```java
import java.util.InputMismatchException;
import java.util.Scanner;

public class RetrieveInputExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);
        int weight;
        int height;
        float bmi;

        System.out.print("Enter your weight (kg): ");
        if (!scanner.hasNextInt()) {
            System.err.println("Invalid input! Please enter a valid integer.");
            scanner.next(); // consume non-integer token
            return; // exit the program
        }
        weight = scanner.nextInt();

        System.out.print("Enter your height (m): ");
        if (!scanner.hasNextFloat()) {
            System.err.println("Invalid input! Please enter a valid floating-point number.");
            scanner.next(); // consume non-float token
            return; // exit the program
        }
        height = (int) Math.round(scanner.nextFloat()); // round height to nearest integer

        bmi = weight / (height * height);
        System.out.printf("Your BMI is %.2f.", bmi);

        scanner.close();
    }
}
```

In this updated version, we check whether the next token in the input stream is an integer or a floating-point number using `Scanner#hasNextInt()` and `Scanner#hasNextFloat()`, respectively. If the expected type of input is not found, we print an error message and consume the invalid token using `Scanner#next()`. We also close the `Scanner` object at the end of the program to prevent resource leakage. This should help improve the overall robustness and usability of the program.

